      <workList xml:id="workDesc_fec7814f">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e229226">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_27e2b4b6">A1:013</identifier>
            <title xml:id="_19" xml:lang="it">Concerto per il cembalo</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_c238e426">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="_21">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_22_0">
               <date notafter="1727-07" xml:id="date_dea25cb8">before July 1727</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_46741069">Leipzig,</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_22_0">
               <p xml:id="idm84">The concerto for solo harpsichord belongs to one of Scheibe’s earliest surviving works, presumably from his time in Leipzig around 1727. It appears in a collection of keyboard works, which once belonged to <persName xml:id="idm85" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Heinrich Nicolaus Gerber</persName> who around that time studied with <persName xml:id="idm86" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Sebastian Bach</persName>. The manuscript also includes works by composers such as Gerber's teacher in addition to <persName xml:id="idm87" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Pachebel</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm88" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Heinrich Buttstett</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm89" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Valentin Eckelt</persName>. Scheibe might indeed have been inspired by <persName xml:id="idm90" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Antonio Vivaldi</persName>'s concertos which Bach transcribed for keyboard and presumably employed in his teaching. There is also evidence suggesting that Scheibe carried out such transcriptions during his early years in Leipzig (cf. SchW A6:001).</p>
            </history>
            <langUsage xml:id="langUsage_08cf91dc">
               <language xml:id="da">Danish</language>
               <language xml:id="en">English</language>
               <language xml:id="it">Italian</language>
            </langUsage>
